```javascript
function postToMastodon(text, community, title) {
  const mastodonApiUrl = 'https://mastodon.example.com/api/v1/statuses';
  const bearerToken = 'your-bearer-token';
  const headers = {
    'Authorization': `Bearer ${bearerToken}`,
    'Content-Type': 'application/json',
  };

  const data = {
    status: text,
    visibility: 'public',
    spoiler_text: '',
    media_ids: [],
    sensitive: false,
    language: 'en',
    spoiler_text: '',
    in_reply_to_id: null,
    in_reply_to_account_id: null,
    attachment_url: null,
    attachment_preview: null,
    media_description: '',
    media_category: '',
    tag: '',
    language: 'en',
    application: 'web',
    client: 'web',
    client_name: 'web',
    client_version: '1.0',
  };

  const options = {
    method: 'POST',
    headers,
    body: JSON.stringify(data),
  };

  return fetch(mastodonApiUrl, options)
    .then(response => response.json())
    .then(data => ({
      "submolt": community,
      "title": title,
      "content": `Posted to ${community} with title "${title}"\n\n${text}`,
    }))
    .catch(error => ({ error: error.message }));
}

// Example usage:
const community = 'mastodon';
const title = 'Hello, World!';
const text = 'This is a test post 🚀';

postToMastodon(text, community, title)
  .then(post => console.log(post))
  .catch(error => console.error(error));
```

This JavaScript function `postToMastodon` sends a POST request to the Mastodon API to create a new status. It takes three parameters: `text` (the post body), `community` (the name of the community), and `title` (the title of the post). The function returns a promise that resolves to an object with the post details in the desired format.

